###
## MSMTP + mailx setup for Debian 12 (bookworm) / Debian 13 (trixie)
##
## Goal:
## - local scripts and system services (cron, sudo, PAM hooks,
##   unattended-upgrades, rkhunter, mdadm, ...) can send notification emails
## - no full MTA (Postfix/Exim) needed, no listening daemon, no open port
## - an external SMTP relay is used (for example Gmail)
##
## Used packages:
## - msmtp       : SMTP client that sends mail to the relay
## - msmtp-mta   : provides /usr/sbin/sendmail compatibility
## - bsd-mailx   : provides the `mail` command
##
## Limitations (know them before relying on this setup):
## - NO QUEUE: if the relay is unreachable at send time, the mail is lost.
##   Mitigation: send a daily "heartbeat" mail; if it does not arrive,
##   either the host or mail delivery is broken.
## - Gmail rewrites the From address to the authenticated account.
##   Put the hostname into the subject line to identify the sender host.
## - With /etc/msmtprc mode 0640 root:root only root can send mail
##   (see section 3).
##
## Debian 12 vs 13: the steps below are the same on both releases.
## Release-specific notes are marked with "[Debian 12]" / "[Debian 13]".
###


###
## 0. Backup existing files
##
## Convention: <file>.bkp.yyyymmddhhmm next to the original.
## Safe here: msmtp reads only /etc/msmtprc and the aliases file named in it,
## AppArmor only loads the local file included by name.
###
TS=$(date +%Y%m%d%H%M)
for f in /etc/aliases /etc/msmtprc /etc/apparmor.d/local/usr.bin.msmtp; do
    [ -f "$f" ] && cp -a "$f" "$f.bkp.$TS"
done


###
## 1. Install packages
##
## Notes:
## - msmtp-mta conflicts with other MTAs: installing it REMOVES an existing
##   exim4 / postfix (exim4 is often pulled in as a dependency on a fresh
##   install, listening on localhost:25). Do not do this on a host that must
##   keep its full MTA.
## - The msmtp package asks (debconf) whether to enable its AppArmor profile.
##   Answer YES; then section 5 is mandatory.
###
apt install msmtp msmtp-mta bsd-mailx


###
## 2. Configure aliases
##
## File:
## - /etc/aliases (template: etc_aliases in this repo)
##
## Notes:
## - msmtp reads this file directly (see `aliases` in /etc/msmtprc)
## - `newaliases` is not needed in this setup
## - local system recipients resolve to root
## - root resolves to a real external email address
## - replace ADMINUSER with the host's admin account name
## - use either a local alias name (root) or a full email address as target,
##   never something like `root@`
###
cat > /etc/aliases <<'EOF'
# Local mail recipients, used by msmtp (see /etc/msmtprc)
# Fallback for any recipient not listed below
default: email@address.tld
mailer-daemon: root
postmaster: root
nobody: root
hostmaster: root
usenet: root
news: root
webmaster: root
www: root
ftp: root
abuse: root
noc: root
security: root
# Admin account of this host
ADMINUSER: root
root: email@address.tld
EOF


###
## 3. Configure MSMTP
##
## File:
## - /etc/msmtprc
##
## Permissions:
## - 0640 root:root (effectively the same as 0600): only root can read the
##   config, so only processes running as root can send mail.
##   This is intentional: it keeps the relay password away from other users.
##   Typical senders (cron jobs of root, sudo, PAM hooks, unattended-upgrades,
##   rkhunter, mdadm) all run as root.
##   If a non-root script must send mail, run it via root's cron or sudo
##   instead of loosening these permissions.
##
## Notes:
## - Gmail requires an application password (the normal account password
##   does not work); this needs 2-step verification on the Google account
## - for Gmail, `user` must be the full email address
## - `from` should match the authenticated account
## - Logging alternative: instead of `logfile` you can use `syslog LOG_MAIL`
##   and read the log with `journalctl -t msmtp`. Then sections 4, 5 and 6
##   are not needed. The logfile variant below keeps a separate, rotated file.
###
cat > /etc/msmtprc <<'EOF'
## Default settings
defaults
auth           on
tls            on
tls_trust_file /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt
logfile        /var/log/msmtp/msmtp.log
# Map local recipients (root, admin user, ...) to external addresses
aliases        /etc/aliases

## Gmail account
account        gmail
host           smtp.gmail.com
port           587
from           youraccount@gmail.com
user           youraccount@gmail.com
password       YOUR_GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD

## Default account
account default : gmail
EOF

chown root:root /etc/msmtprc
chmod 0640 /etc/msmtprc


###
## 4. Create log path
##
## Important:
## - msmtp does NOT run as a daemon
## - it runs as the user invoking `mail` / `sendmail`, here always root
## - group adm allows admins to read the log without root
###
mkdir -p /var/log/msmtp
touch /var/log/msmtp/msmtp.log
chown root:adm /var/log/msmtp /var/log/msmtp/msmtp.log
chmod 0755 /var/log/msmtp
chmod 0640 /var/log/msmtp/msmtp.log


###
## 5. AppArmor
##
## AppArmor is enabled by default on Debian 12 and 13.
## If the msmtp profile is in enforce mode, it does NOT allow writing to
## /var/log/msmtp/. Mail is still delivered, but the log stays empty,
## which is easy to miss. So check this BEFORE testing, not only on error.
##
## 5.1 Check whether the profile is loaded and enforced
###
aa-status | sed -n '/enforce mode/,/complain mode/p' | grep msmtp
# Output "msmtp" (and "msmtp//helpers") -> enforced, continue with 5.2
# No output -> profile not enforced (AppArmor support declined at install),
#              skip the rest of section 5

###
## 5.2 Check the name of the local override file
##
## Note: in AppArmor profiles `#include <...>` (with the hash) is the old
## include syntax and is ACTIVE, not a comment.
## Do NOT edit the main profile; package updates would overwrite it.
###
grep -n 'local' /etc/apparmor.d/usr.bin.msmtp
# Expected: #include <local/usr.bin.msmtp>

###
## 5.3 Allow the log directory and reload the profile
###
mkdir -p /etc/apparmor.d/local
cat > /etc/apparmor.d/local/usr.bin.msmtp <<'EOF'
# Allow msmtp to write its own log directory (see logfile in /etc/msmtprc)
/var/log/msmtp/ wk,
/var/log/msmtp/** wk,
EOF

apparmor_parser -r /etc/apparmor.d/usr.bin.msmtp


###
## 6. Logrotate
##
## File:
## - /etc/logrotate.d/msmtp (template: msmtp_logrotate.d in this repo)
##
## Notes:
## - msmtp opens the logfile on every send, so no postrotate action is needed
## - ownership matches section 4
###
cat > /etc/logrotate.d/msmtp <<'EOF'
/var/log/msmtp/msmtp.log
{
	rotate 7
	weekly
	missingok
	notifempty
	compress
	delaycompress
	create 0640 root adm
}
EOF

logrotate --debug /etc/logrotate.d/msmtp


###
## 7. Test commands
##
## Test direct external recipient
###
echo "MSMTP test message from $(hostname -f) at $(date)" | mail -s "[$(hostname -s)] msmtp test" you@example.com

###
## Test alias resolution through root
###
echo "MSMTP test message from $(hostname -f) at $(date)" | mail -s "[$(hostname -s)] msmtp test" root

###
## Check the log
###
tail -n 5 /var/log/msmtp/msmtp.log

###
## Optional, only on hosts with software RAID: mdadm monitor test
###
# mdadm --monitor --scan --test --oneshot


###
## 8. Expected behavior
##
## Successful SMTP delivery is indicated in the log by:
## - smtpstatus=250
## - exitcode=EX_OK
## and the mail arrives at the address set for root in /etc/aliases.
##
## Mail can be delivered successfully while logging fails
## (AppArmor or file permissions). An empty log after a successful test
## means section 4 or 5 is wrong.
###


###
## 9. Troubleshooting
##
## Problem:
## - `mail: command not found`
## Fix:
## - install bsd-mailx
##
## Problem:
## - mail is delivered, but the log stays empty or shows
##   "cannot log to /var/log/msmtp/msmtp.log: Permission denied"
## Fix:
## - check ownership and mode on /var/log/msmtp and msmtp.log (section 4)
## - check AppArmor denials:
##     journalctl -k | grep -i 'apparmor="DENIED"' | grep msmtp
## - extend the local AppArmor profile and reload it (section 5)
##
## Problem:
## - alias does not work
## Fix:
## - verify /etc/aliases syntax (one "name: target" per line)
## - do not use invalid targets like `root@`
## - use either a local alias name (`root`) or a full email address
##
## Problem:
## - Gmail authentication fails
## Fix:
## - use a Gmail application password
## - use the full Gmail address as `user`
##
## Problem:
## - a non-root user or script cannot send mail
##   ("cannot read /etc/msmtprc" or similar)
## Fix:
## - expected with 0640 root:root (section 3); run the sender as root
##
## Problem:
## - an expected mail never arrived, no error visible
## Fix:
## - there is no queue: check the log at the time of sending for a failure
##   (relay unreachable, TLS or auth error); the mail must be resent
###


###
## 10. Minimal rule for scripts
##
## For scripts using:
## - mail -s "subject" recipient < bodyfile
## - or /usr/sbin/sendmail directly
##
## this package set is sufficient:
## - msmtp
## - msmtp-mta
## - bsd-mailx
##
## No full MTA is needed.
###
